::: {.callout-note appearance="simple" icon="false"}
## Learning objectives

By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

- write the simple linear regression model and state its LINE assumptions;
- fit the model in R and interpret the intercept and slope in context;
- partition variability with ANOVA and interpret the MSE and $R^2$;
- test the slope with a t-test and the overall model with an F-test;
- tell a confidence interval for the mean response apart from a prediction interval;
- check the assumptions with residual diagnostics and influence measures.
:::

## Introduction

Simple Linear Regression (SLR) is one of the most fundamental and widely used tools in epidemiology and public health research. It allows us to:

- **Quantify** the linear relationship between a continuous outcome and a single predictor
- **Predict** values of an outcome given a predictor value
- **Test hypotheses** about whether a predictor is associated with an outcome
- **Lay the groundwork** for multiple regression, which controls for confounding

In epidemiology, we frequently use SLR to model continuous outcomes such as blood pressure, BMI, cholesterol levels, or hospital length of stay as a function of age, exposure levels, or other continuous predictors.

## Setup and Data


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(tidyverse)
library(brfssdata)
library(knitr)
library(kableExtra)
library(plotly)
library(broom)
library(ggeffects)
library(gtsummary)
```
:::


### Loading BRFSS 2020 Data

We will use the **Behavioral Risk Factor Surveillance System (BRFSS) 2020** data throughout this lecture. The BRFSS is a large-scale, nationally representative telephone survey conducted by the CDC that collects data on health behaviors, chronic conditions, and preventive service use among U.S. adults.

::: {.column-margin}
BRFSS 2020 codebook: [CDC annual data page](https://www.cdc.gov/brfss/annual_data/annual_2020.html). Package documentation: [brfssdata](https://muntasirmasum.github.io/brfssdata/).
:::

::: {.data-card}
[Data for this lecture]{.eyebrow}

**BRFSS 2020, loaded with the brfssdata R package.** The first time you run the code below, `read_brfss()` downloads the 2020 survey (about 21 MB) and keeps it on your computer, so later runs read it from there. We then draw a random sample of 3,000 adults aged 18 to 80 with complete data on BMI, age, sex, education, general health, sleep, and days of poor physical health.

Install the packages once with `install.packages(c("brfssdata", "janitor"))`.
:::

The full BRFSS 2020 file has 401,958 respondents. We read only the seven variables this lecture needs, recode them, and draw the analytic sample. Because the code sets a seed, everyone who runs it gets the same 3,000 people.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# Read seven BRFSS 2020 variables (CDC names; underscore names need quotes)
brfss_raw <- read_brfss(
  2020,
  vars = c("_BMI5", "_AGE80", "_SEX", "_EDUCAG", "GENHLTH", "PHYSHLTH", "SLEPTIM1")
) |>
  janitor::clean_names()

# Recode variables
brfss_slr <- brfss_raw |>
  select(bmi5, age80, sex, educag, genhlth, physhlth, sleptim1) |>
  mutate(
    bmi       = bmi5 / 100,
    age       = age80,
    sex       = factor(ifelse(sex == 1, "Male", "Female")),
    education = factor(case_when(
      educag == 1 ~ "< High school",
      educag == 2 ~ "High school graduate",
      educag == 3 ~ "Some college",
      educag == 4 ~ "College graduate"
    ), levels = c("< High school", "High school graduate",
                  "Some college", "College graduate")),
    gen_health_num = ifelse(genhlth  %in% 1:5,  genhlth,  NA_real_),
    sleep_hrs      = ifelse(sleptim1 %in% 1:24, sleptim1, NA_real_),
    phys_days      = ifelse(physhlth %in% 0:30, physhlth, NA_real_)
  )

# Select recoded variables, apply filters, drop missing, take sample
set.seed(553)
brfss_slr <- brfss_slr |>
  select(bmi, age, sex, education, gen_health_num, sleep_hrs, phys_days) |>
  filter(bmi > 14.5, bmi < 60, age >= 18, age <= 80) |>
  drop_na() |>
  slice_sample(n = 3000)

tibble(
  Metric = c("Observations", "Variables"),
  Value  = c(nrow(brfss_slr), ncol(brfss_slr))
) |>
  kable(caption = "Analytic Dataset Dimensions") |>
  kable_styling(bootstrap_options = "striped", full_width = FALSE)
```

