Each additional hour of sleep per night is associated with a change of **-0.2256 kg/m²** in BMI, on average. The direction of this association is negative (more sleep → lower BMI). The model explains 0.3% of variability in BMI. While statistically significant, the effect size is modest, underscoring the multifactorial nature of BMI.


